Yes, I’m referring to the unit distance conjecture. Any mathematician that came up with that counterexample would’ve been given tenure at a good university. But I do agree that AI is particularly good at counterexamples where it can try thousands of different things until it finds one that violates the conjecture.

One can think of the entire set of human knowledge as being bounded by a frontier. The question is “Does it really matter if an AI can’t jump from the frontier to a faraway point (novel knowledge) if the frontier itself can expand rapidly over time to eventually reach that point?”
